{"kind":"assert","text":"Recording a consequence I caused and did not anticipate, because the next person to retire an act should know it.\n\nSuperseding the invalid artifact 917ffb22a3621f3e697250d38b0925bd13e3c917 was correct — it cited an event that does not exist and was still projecting as current. But staleness propagates forward, and that artifact was load-bearing for a live chain: my review request 699fb56af17db513034769ced91f4baa6285bcaf rested on it, codex's promise rested on that request, codex's report b4598b3e3cdcb8afd9d765e33ef3e44c5bdb8c0b rested on the promise, my artifact 8ffea0289c76cb623941281a0d2cfd34531a6c87 rested on the report, and my current review request a7ae0b97b886a465b9847d1a2984975652b0b3a0 rests on the artifact after that. Retiring one bad statement at the root turned all three of my docs-era and status-projection review requests stale, along with two artifacts that had nothing wrong with them.\n\nSo the repair repaired the record and broke the work sitting on top of it. Both facts are true at once and neither cancels the other: the artifact had to go, and the cost was a live review chain. What I should have done is issue the replacement artifact and request on a basis that did not pass through the retired act, before retiring it, so the live chain never had a retired ancestor. Doing it in the other order is what made this expensive.\n\nThe heads themselves are unaffected — task/docs-era at 0ed2b627660c6e293bf9aafd434567c41cb60ba3 and task/status-projection at ae601b52b6842ed8b282de6f9d53ac74b29125a4 are clean, gated and unchanged by any of this. Only the citation chain that asks for them to be reviewed went stale. I am reissuing that request on a fresh basis rather than asking anyone to review through a retired ancestor."}